    "q-319824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q319824",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x+1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)+1}{1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
